# Learn rat-stack from your own agent

Learn mode explains the ideas behind rat-stack-shaped apps while your own agent does real work. It goes deeper the first time you meet an idea and gets shorter as you learn it.

## Turn it on

Copy this prompt into your agent. The agent asks you to confirm before it starts.

<CopyPrompt id="learn" />

The first path starts with [why dependency injection is the reason to choose Effect](/lore/dependency-injection-is-the-reason-to-choose-effect).

## How deep it goes

- A new concept gets one walkthrough.
- An introduced concept gets a line, or a paragraph after seven days.
- A familiar concept gets a line after seven days.
- A fluent concept stays silent unless you ask, or after ninety days of inactivity.
- A dismissed concept stays silent.

## What is stored and where

Progress stays on your machine in `~/.rat-learn/`. Only the learn tools write this folder.

- `log.jsonl` holds the learning events. It is the source.
- `cards.jsonl` holds concept progress, rebuilt from the log.
- `tasks.jsonl` holds concept ids and depths, rebuilt from the log.

The records hold concept ids, timestamps, depth, familiarity, and dismissal state.

## What is never sent

- Your code, file paths, names, repository names, and prompts.
- Your progress. The public deck and card tools need no progress, and they store nothing.

## Turn it off

- Say "learn mode off". The agent stops learning tool calls and explanations.
- Your progress stays. Turning the mode off does not erase it.
- Ask the agent to dismiss one concept to stop seeing that concept.

